#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a99a198-0f4f-5492-b567-df73208d6cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a99a198-0f4f-5492-b567-df73208d6cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256716} :answer-field/id #uuid "07aa1240-3085-53f7-95b8-d6b037a48ffb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c205fa0-d12d-510f-bf38-5376ffd48764" :question/math-academy-id "q-107499" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41fa9c06-6750-5d76-a1fb-74763af261b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41fa9c06-6750-5d76-a1fb-74763af261b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1274f57-0d34-5aee-9ab9-5b91fb8f9816" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b3de9cf-84d9-5ffb-a26d-3666f3702b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-110799" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}(2x+4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(4) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(8) & =\\operatorname{log}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c5a147-0100-5f4b-a221-25b8674b2115" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Sum of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a6ab14-c083-5397-b5c0-92247b539573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57a6ab14-c083-5397-b5c0-92247b539573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257075} :answer-field/id #uuid "b703f3c5-9f22-567a-aa37-1280b069e67d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e33d82b-67b7-5f1d-84c4-6afcc46ec7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114209" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}1-\\operatorname{arctan}0)+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed1761-ceeb-5033-afa2-cb735e6bb96c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f74fd72-dd28-524a-9c7f-1084f8649375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f74fd72-dd28-524a-9c7f-1084f8649375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257118} :answer-field/id #uuid "d452168e-bfbc-5305-927c-94998c46c6d4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bd10278-9b76-5fcf-980b-136026be1670" :question/math-academy-id "q-114961" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d26e455a-be78-54a6-8ee0-868b455d0058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d26e455a-be78-54a6-8ee0-868b455d0058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257136} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a1bb95-efd6-5816-8d00-b1ae1239d5ad" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c976869-dd96-5219-84a7-962a7edc295e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115511" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e66950-5962-5b82-a7a0-6b569173657e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e66950-5962-5b82-a7a0-6b569173657e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257388} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c2566c-62b1-5822-b528-94b1c53921a7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a918518-91f1-50bb-bef6-5c2c5dd04e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119865" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}x={sin}^{2}x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edfcc133-8b6e-5eb1-9ec7-b30dc4894296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edfcc133-8b6e-5eb1-9ec7-b30dc4894296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257412} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdcef4a3-0af8-5150-93fe-2db0d581e203" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b74e706-500e-512a-b8da-4eca1cba7d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b74e706-500e-512a-b8da-4eca1cba7d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257414} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d02f2f9-f771-5b05-b5f5-789f22e55ceb" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b65dbd4c-66de-5fa9-bf68-86d78bdb1d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-120300" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-11).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-11)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121} \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe335eb-dc4a-5ea0-83cf-ad929e19b908" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection and Shifting of a Sine or Cosine Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d0a0c8-61e7-511c-8ae9-44d1ee078f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15d0a0c8-61e7-511c-8ae9-44d1ee078f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257807} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aeac9b3-1f57-55e5-93b8-119ce7e31478" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af276f5b-4246-5be5-bc99-ae92802b70cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-12669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e92694d3fc8d6be220fc4d45c0d210a53076db72daad477c3199ece5c4460627.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258092} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e30f645-54b0-5eae-b57e-34f9c4ffffff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00670c27-9d46-5e38-8a9d-ebcbc2c333bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00670c27-9d46-5e38-8a9d-ebcbc2c333bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259103} :answer-field/id #uuid "441c5a2e-f151-5073-9f10-4c3c8e6785a8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ba40cf-c229-5faa-87a1-5d86079dac01" :question/math-academy-id "q-26387" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of ${log}_{9},$ what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${9}^{2y+1}={5}^{-y}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641d7778-b4a5-5a1f-9803-f84355fa85f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641d7778-b4a5-5a1f-9803-f84355fa85f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfbbf58-a0ba-5f08-ad19-4b0db32db4a1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34caa26-5f27-577f-8e03-422281cd8826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a34caa26-5f27-577f-8e03-422281cd8826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261492} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c45fb31-4247-5b73-97e3-1ea727fd41cd" :answer-field/key "time-s" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261491} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97d4513-2be1-5a3e-adfd-fc5458c9499b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c97d4513-2be1-5a3e-adfd-fc5458c9499b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261494} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c23dbb-d50f-502d-9f65-2aa430c4048d" :answer-field/key "velocity-m-per-s" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nTime s: {{time-s}}\n\nVelocity m per s: {{velocity-m-per-s}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe0d77e-52ec-55af-86ee-7865dc316245" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Monomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad687d2-f64e-5027-8c29-e8305e93f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ad687d2-f64e-5027-8c29-e8305e93f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261613} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a00dc30-5eb1-5d80-ac45-2d5ccaa66e8e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb1d235-c690-5225-8779-e89c17dee989" :question/math-academy-id "q-76311" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $6w,$ $3{w}^{3},$ and $4{w}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three monomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable parts. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4):$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,12,18,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,6,9,12,…$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,8,12,16,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4)=12.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $w,\\,{w}^{3},$ and ${w}^{2},$ we raise $w$ to the largest power that appears in all the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,{w}^{3},{w}^{2})={w}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6w,3{w}^{3},4{w}^{2})=12\\cdot {w}^{3}=12{w}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f783fe8-8f52-5f93-adef-bf4bd030fea0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Vertical Hyperbola in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa18e828-2032-5e08-b585-b038a583bdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa18e828-2032-5e08-b585-b038a583bdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261706} :answer-field/id #uuid "417ff34e-05a0-579a-a5a1-7bcf45081217" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa7228ce-645a-52a8-9a40-e535c79572db" :question/math-academy-id "q-78257" :question/problem "Write the equation of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=40$ in standard form.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $40$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb365004-d46f-54d9-bff4-8a747d30edc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb365004-d46f-54d9-bff4-8a747d30edc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261762} :answer-field/id #uuid "442bb6ce-c15e-566c-8b57-df0b61c03012" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28eb4849-33b4-5a68-b600-726a71471105" :question/math-academy-id "q-79870" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}(-3x+2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(8) & ={log}_{4}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(5) & ={log}_{4}(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eab4249-544e-56c6-81c0-e6db84033701" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance Given the Number, Sum, and Sum of Squares of the Data Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261940} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-87562" :question/problem "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
